          lemma: "τίς ἥμαρτεν … ἵνα φανερωθῇ",
          title: "“Who sinned?” — the wrong question",
          body: "The disciples assume what everyone assumed: suffering must be *punishment*, so a man born blind means someone sinned (his own prenatal sin, or his parents’). Jesus refuses the whole calculus — **neither** — and reframes the question entirely. He does not say *why* the man is blind (there is no neat answer here to the mystery of suffering); he says what God will *do* with it: **“that the works of God might be displayed in him.”** Not that God blinded him for a demonstration, but that this affliction, whatever its cause, becomes the very place where God’s light breaks in. It is the pastoral heart of the chapter: the question is not *whose fault?* but *what is God about to do?*",
