      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a9.22.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g9.22.21'],
          lemma: "ἀποσυνάγωγος",
          title: "Put out of the synagogue",
          body: "For a Jewish family, expulsion from the **synagogue** (**ἀποσυνάγωγος**, a rare word John uses three times, 9:22; 12:42; 16:2) meant being cut off from the centre of communal, religious, and economic life — a social death. John, writing decades later, likely reflects the real cost his own community paid for confessing Jesus. Notice the contrast the chapter builds: the parents, gripped by this fear, keep their distance from their own healed son; the man, with nothing to lose but honesty, keeps stepping *toward* the truth — and is duly thrown out. Fear shrinks the parents; courage enlarges the beggar.",
        },
      ],
